==English==
{{was wotd|2006|April|22}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|ML.|-}} {{m|la|prognosticare}}; see {{m|en|prognostic}} for more.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-prognosticate.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{IPA|en|/pɹɒɡˈnɒstɪkeɪt/}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[predict]] or [[forecast]], especially through the application of skill.
#: {{syn|en|foretell|prophesy}}
#: {{ux|en|Examining the tea-leaves, she '''prognosticated''' dark days ahead.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|14|text=But from thine eyes my knowledge I derive,<br>And constant stars in them I read such art<br>As 'Truth and beauty shall together thrive,<br>If from thyself, to store thou wouldst convert';<br>Or else of thee this I '''prognosticate''':<br>'Thy end is truth's and beauty's doom and date.'}}
#* {{RQ:Emily Bronte Wuthering Heights|VII|text=...to-morrow I intend lengthening the night till afternoon. I '''prognosticate''' for myself an obstinate cold, at least.}}
#* '''1915''' – [[w:Virginia Woolf|Virginia Woolf]], [http://www.gutenberg.org/files/144/144-h/144-h.htm|''The Voyage Out''] ch. 2
#*: All old people and many sick people were drawn, were it only for a foot or two, into the open air, and '''prognosticated''' pleasant things about the course of the world.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[presage]], [[betoken]].
#: {{syn|en|foretoken|foreshadow|portend}}
#: {{ux|en|The bluebells may '''prognosticate''' an early spring this year.}}

